        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 md:p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Mass gainer vs protein powder comes down to one question: is your problem calories or protein? A gainer like Serious Mass delivers about 1,250 calories per full serving, mostly from maltodextrin, at about $5.27 per 1,000 calories but about $3.30 per 25 g of protein. Whey like Gold Standard delivers 24 g of protein for about 120 calories at about $1.49 per 25 g. Most people trying to gain do best with whey blended into milk, oats and peanut butter.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy a gainer if you truly cannot eat more food, start with half a serving and watch the scale. Buy protein powder if you can eat but miss your protein, want leaner gains or need to manage blood sugar. Whichever you choose, train hard, aim for a modest surplus, and check with a doctor or dietitian first if you have diabetes, kidney disease or another medical condition.
          </p>
